<description>
**Problem:** The coins cache still exposed and tested states that production code should not create: standalone `FRESH` entries, non-dirty `BatchWrite()` cursor entries, and spent `FRESH` entries.
That makes the representation harder to reason about, because `CCoinsCacheEntry` stores a separate `DIRTY` bit even though dirty entries are already tracked in the linked list.

**Fix:** This revives and splits the relevant parts of #30673 and #33018.
The series passes freshness through `SetDirty()`, removes fresh-only test states, treats `BatchWrite()` cursor entries as dirty by construction, rejects spent `FRESH` entries, removes the bare `SetFresh()` helper, and derives dirtiness from linked-list membership.
The final commits also tighten `SpendCoin()` around the same cache-state contract: repeated spends of an entry already cached as spent now fail without changing cache accounting or caller-owned output, and callers must either check the result or explicitly discard it.
`coinscache_sim` now checks the real `SpendCoin()` result against its simulation model, and the no-op repeated-spend path no longer emits the `utxocache:spent` tracepoint because no spend occurs.
</description>

<commits>
c19068f1fe test: characterize coin cache state handling

Limit fixtures to entries that are dirty whenever they are fresh, and prepare the fuzz harness to avoid spent fresh entries and duplicate insertion accounting.
Record that repeated spends report success and overwrite caller-owned output, while retaining the existing state and accounting checks.

Co-authored-by: Lőrinc <pap.lorinc@gmail.com>

2444634014 coins: pass freshness through `SetDirty()`

Fresh entries must also be dirty.
Require the resulting freshness at every `SetDirty()` call and remove the bare `SetFresh()` helper, so callers cannot create a fresh entry without linking it into the dirty list.

Co-authored-by: Andrew Toth <andrewstoth@gmail.com>

6a6c290079 coins: assert dirty `BatchWrite()` cursor entries

`CoinsViewCacheCursor` iterates the dirty-entry list.
Treat cursor entries as dirty instead of checking them again, while retaining assertions of the cursor contract.

Co-authored-by: Andrew Toth <andrewstoth@gmail.com>

b7f35ad836 coins: reject spent FRESH entries in BatchWrite()

Spending a fresh entry erases it locally because the parent never knew it existed.
Reject a spent fresh entry before merging it into the parent cache instead of treating it as a valid state.

28ead9c57f refactor: inline dirty-list insertion

`SetDirty()` is the only remaining caller of `AddFlags()`.
Inline the helper so the representation change is confined to the entry's state management.

90685bd555 coins: derive `DIRTY` from linked list membership

List membership already identifies dirty entries, so the separate `DIRTY` bit duplicates state.
Store only freshness and require it to be preserved while an entry remains dirty.

Co-authored-by: Andrew Toth <andrewstoth@gmail.com>

c14d63a15c coins: fail `SpendCoin()` on already-spent entries

Return false without overwriting caller-owned output when an entry is already cached as spent.
Cache accounting was already unchanged for reachable spent entries.
Production callers retain their behavior: checked inputs remain spendable, disconnects report unclean state, and replay ignores repeated deletes.
The rejected spend no longer emits the `utxocache:spent` tracepoint.

6fd59bf706 coins: require checking `SpendCoin()` results

Mark `SpendCoin()` as `[[nodiscard]]` so accidental ignored results are rejected.
The mempool consistency check requires successful spends, while replay explicitly ignores repeated deletes after an interrupted coins DB flush.
</commits>

